  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Spotty minor tidal flooding expected with this \nmorning's high tide. \n\nElevated astronomical tides from the recent full moon and \nnortheast onshore flow resulted in some spotty minor tidal \nflooding across portions of southern Atlantic coastal New \nJersey and Delaware, and Delaware Bay early Wednesday morning. \nFor those same areas, another round of spotty minor tidal \nflooding is forecast with this morning's high tide cycle. \nHowever, tide levels should be slightly lower than this morning,\nand no advisories are warranted at this time.\n\nNo tidal flooding is expected on the tidal Delaware River, \nnorthern New Jersey coast, or Chesapeake Bay communities.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Areas of fog and low clouds anticipated this \nmorning. Otherwise, no other significant weather impacts with \ngenerally tranquil conditions through early next week.\n\nLatest trends and guidance support increased confidence in \nareas of fog and/or low stratus developing through daybreak this \nmorning. Skies have cleared and winds have decoupled. Some fog \nand low stratus have already developed, and this is anticipated \nto continue increasing in coverage through dawn. Cannot totally \nrule out some locally dense fog, though current guidance \nsuggests widespread dense fog is unlikely. Will continue to \nmonitor.\n\nHigh pressure will shift offshore through today, then a dry cold\nfront is expected to push through the region and offshore Friday\nmorning. High pressure will then return through early next week\nwith continued mild and dry conditions expected.\n\nA couple of warmer days to close out the week ahead, with high\ntemperatures in the upper 60s to mid 70s in most areas.\nFollowing any morning fog and low clouds, skies will turn partly\ncloudy to mostly sunny this afternoon with a light southwest\nbreeze. Mild tonight with lows in the upper 40s to low 50s.\nFriday will turn breezy in the wake of the morning cold frontal\npassage. Northwest winds 10-15 mph with gusts up to 25 mph can\nbe expected.\n\nConditions will turn cooler again for the weekend. Lows Friday\nnight through Sunday night will be in the mid 30s to mid 40s,\nbut frost currently appears unlikely where the growing season\ncontinues. Highs will be coolest on Halloween, in the upper 50s\nto mid 60s, under partly cloudy skies. Partly to mostly cloudy\nskies Sunday, but milder with highs in the mid 60s for most.\n\nThe system which had potential to affect the region early next \nweek now appears to weaken and miss our region for the most \npart. This will keep our region dry, but still increase cloud \ncover. We cannot totally rule out a few showers midweek, but the\nnext period to keep an eye on for widespread rainfall looks to \nbe toward the end of next week, when guidance suggests a more \nprolific system may impact the region.",
